=encoding utf8

=head1 NAME

SDL3 - low-level binding generated from the C headers

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  use SDL3 ':all';            # everything
  use SDL3 ':video';          # one header's functions and constants
  use SDL3 qw(SDL_Init);      # single symbols

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Loads the XS code and re-exports every function and constant of the
per-header modules. Tags are available per header (C<:assert> C<:asyncio> C<:atomic> C<:audio> C<:bits> C<:blendmode> C<:camera> C<:clipboard> C<:cpuinfo> C<:dialog> C<:dlopennote> C<:endian> C<:error> C<:events> C<:filesystem> C<:gamepad> C<:gpu> C<:guid> C<:haptic> C<:hidapi> C<:hints> C<:init> C<:iostream> C<:joystick> C<:keyboard> C<:keycode> C<:loadso> C<:locale> C<:log> C<:messagebox> C<:metal> C<:misc> C<:mouse> C<:mutex> C<:pen> C<:pixels> C<:platform> C<:platform_defines> C<:power> C<:process> C<:properties> C<:rect> C<:render> C<:scancode> C<:sensor> C<:storage> C<:surface> C<:system> C<:thread> C<:time> C<:timer> C<:touch> C<:tray> C<:version> C<:video> )
as well as C<:functions>, C<:constants> and C<:all>.

=head1 CONVENTIONS

=over

=item Strings

Every C<char *> result is a Perl string (UTF-8 both ways); undef is NULL.
Results the library hands over to the caller are freed by the binding right
after copying, so nothing has to be released from Perl. A non-const
C<char *> parameter is a writable buffer: pass a scalar sized beforehand,
e.g. C<< "\0" x 64 >>.

=item Structs

Pointers to structs and unions are objects of a class per type, see
L<SDL3::Struct>. Struct arguments passed by value accept the same objects.

=item Out parameters

A pointer to a scalar or to a pointer accepts a reference to a scalar
(read before, written after the call), a L<SDL3::Pointer>, or undef for NULL.

=item Callbacks

A function pointer parameter accepts a code reference; the accompanying
C<void *userdata> parameter accepts any scalar, which the callback
receives in the same position. The holder keeping both alive is never
freed: registering callbacks repeatedly leaks a few words each time.
Callbacks must be invoked on the Perl thread.

=item Variadic functions

printf-style functions take their fixed arguments except the format
string, plus one trailing message that is printed verbatim.

=back

=cut
